### How does connection pooling cut overhead?

Opening a new connection to an upstream service means a TCP handshake plus a TLS handshake, costing several round trips and CPU. A connection pool keeps connections alive and reuses them. HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests over one connection.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import io.netty.channel.ChannelOption;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import reactor.netty.http.client.HttpClient;
import reactor.netty.resources.ConnectionProvider;
import java.time.Duration;

@Configuration
public class ConnectionPoolConfig {

    @Bean
    public HttpClient httpClient() {
        ConnectionProvider provider = ConnectionProvider.builder("gateway-pool")
                .maxConnections(500)               // Max connections
                .maxIdleTime(Duration.ofSeconds(20)) // Keep-alive
                .maxLifeTime(Duration.ofMinutes(5)) // Recycle connections
                .pendingAcquireTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(500))
                .build();

        return HttpClient.create(provider)
                .option(ChannelOption.CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MILLIS, 1000)
                .responseTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(3000));
    }
}
```

```text
// Without pool: every request = new TCP + TLS handshake = ~5ms overhead
// With pool: >99% of requests reuse existing connections = ~0.05ms
// Savings: 100x reduction in connection setup cost

// HTTP/2 multiplexing: 100 concurrent requests share 1 connection
// Instead of 100 connections, you need ~1-5 connections per backend
```

### How does hybrid rate limiting work?

A naive distributed limiter does a Redis round trip per request. The hybrid approach keeps a local token bucket and syncs with Redis periodically.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.ratelimit;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class HybridRateLimiter {

    private final AtomicInteger localTokens;
    private final int maxTokens;
    private final RedisRateLimitSync redisSync;

    public HybridRateLimiter(int maxTokens, RedisRateLimitSync redisSync) {
        this.maxTokens = maxTokens;
        this.localTokens = new AtomicInteger(maxTokens);
        this.redisSync = redisSync;

        // Sync with Redis every 100ms
        Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor()
                .scheduleAtFixedRate(this::syncWithRedis, 100, 100,
                        TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    }

    public boolean tryAcquire(String clientId) {
        // Local check: instant, no network
        int remaining = localTokens.decrementAndGet();
        return remaining >= 0;
    }

    private void syncWithRedis() {
        // Periodic sync: report local usage, get global remaining
        int consumed = maxTokens - localTokens.get();
        int globalRemaining = redisSync.syncAndGetRemaining(consumed);
        localTokens.set(Math.min(globalRemaining, maxTokens));
    }
}
```

```text
// Naive: 50,000 req/s * 0.5ms Redis call = 25,000ms Redis I/O per second
// Hybrid: 10 syncs/s * 0.5ms = 5ms Redis I/O per second
// Savings: 5,000x reduction in Redis calls
// Trade-off: global count is ~100ms stale between syncs (acceptable)
```

### How does async batched logging work?

Synchronous log writes make the request wait for disk or network I/O. Async logging enqueues events and flushes in batches.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.logging;

import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AsyncAccessLogger {

    private final LinkedBlockingQueue<AccessLogEntry> queue =
            new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(10_000);

    public void log(AccessLogEntry entry) {
        // Non-blocking enqueue: ~0.001ms
        queue.offer(entry);
    }

    // Background worker flushes every 100ms or when batch is full
    public void flush() {
        List<AccessLogEntry> batch = new ArrayList<>();
        queue.drainTo(batch, 1000);
        if (!batch.isEmpty()) {
            writeBatchToDisk(batch); // One disk write for 1000 entries
        }
    }

    private void writeBatchToDisk(List<AccessLogEntry> batch) {
        // Write all entries in one I/O operation
    }
}
```

```text
// Synchronous: 50,000 req/s * 0.3ms per disk write = 15,000ms I/O
// Async batch: 50 flushes/s * 1ms per batch write = 50ms I/O
// Per-request cost: 0.3ms → 0.001ms (300x faster)
// Caution: crash loses buffered entries; for critical audit, use durable queue
```

### How does request coalescing work?

When many identical requests are in flight simultaneously, coalescing makes them share one downstream call instead of each making its own.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

public class RequestCoalescer {

    private final ConcurrentMap<String, Mono<Object>> inFlight =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public Mono<Object> coalesce(String cacheKey,
                                   java.util.function.Supplier<Mono<Object>> fetcher) {
        return inFlight.computeIfAbsent(cacheKey, key -> {
            // First request: create the actual downstream call
            return fetcher.get()
                    .doFinally(signal -> inFlight.remove(key))
                    .cache(); // Share the result with all waiters
        });
    }
}
```

```text
// 100 clients request GET /api/v1/rates/fd simultaneously:
// Without coalescing: 100 calls to rates-service
// With coalescing: 1 call to rates-service, result shared with 100 clients

// IMPORTANT: only for identical, idempotent reads!
// NEVER coalesce: POST /api/v1/transfers (each transfer is unique)
```

### How do these techniques interact?

All four techniques reduce per-request cost independently and stack together.

```text
// Combined savings per request:
// Connection pool: 5ms → 0.05ms (saved 4.95ms)
// Hybrid rate limit: 0.5ms → 0.01ms (saved 0.49ms)
// Async logging: 0.3ms → 0.001ms (saved 0.299ms)
// Request coalescing: N calls → 1 call (saved (N-1) * latency)

// Total per-request cost: ~2ms → ~0.06ms
// Gateway throughput: 20K req/s → 300K+ req/s per replica
```

### What are common mistakes? (wrong vs correct)

```java
// WRONG: connection pool too small → contention
// 10 connections for 50,000 req/s → massive queuing

// CORRECT: size pool based on throughput and upstream capacity
// 500 connections with keep-alive → >99% reuse

// WRONG: coalescing POST /transfers (unique operations!)
// Two different transfers merged → one customer's money moves, other's doesn't

// CORRECT: coalesce only identical, idempotent GETs
// GET /rates/fd → same for everyone → safe to coalesce

// WRONG: unbounded log queue → OOM on spike
// CORRECT: bounded queue with drop policy for overflow
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer measures where per-request time actually goes, applies these techniques in order of impact, and monitors the metrics to validate improvements. They never coalesce state-changing operations.

```text
// Senior optimization checklist:
// 1. Measure: profile per-request time breakdown
// 2. Connection pool: configure size, keep-alive, monitor reuse rate
// 3. Hybrid rate limit: tune sync interval vs precision trade-off
// 4. Async logging: bounded queue, batch size, flush interval
// 5. Coalescing: only identical idempotent reads
// 6. Monitor: connection reuse %, Redis call rate, log flush latency
// 7. The goal: remove any fixed per-request cost at the edge
```

### What are the edge cases?

Connection pooling can backfire if the upstream is a serverless function that resets between invocations, leaving stale connections in the pool. Coalescing must never apply to authenticated or personalized requests because two requests from different users would share results. Hybrid rate limiting drifts by at most one sync interval, which is acceptable for API throttling but not for monetary limits.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Edge case 1: serverless upstream resets connections
        // Pool returns a stale connection -> 502 Bad Gateway
        // Fix: enable health-check pings on idle connections

        // Edge case 2: coalescing authenticated requests
        // WRONG: coalesce GET /api/accounts/me (different per user!)
        // CORRECT: coalesce GET /api/rates/fd (same for everyone)

        // Edge case 3: rate-limit drift
        // Local bucket at 100ms sync: user may get 100ms worth of extra tokens
        // Acceptable for API throttling, NOT for per-second money limits

        System.out.println("Edge case 1: stale pooled connections to serverless");
        System.out.println("Edge case 2: never coalesce user-specific responses");
        System.out.println("Edge case 3: sync drift acceptable for throttling, not money");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Edge case 1: stale pooled connections to serverless
Edge case 2: never coalesce user-specific responses
Edge case 3: sync drift acceptable for throttling, not money
```
